    <section class="lesson-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 · BEFORE INDEPENDENCE</p>
      <h2>Why did the colonies break away from Britain?</h2>
      <p>By the middle of the eighteenth century, thirteen British colonies had developed along the Atlantic coast of North America. They had different economies, local governments, religious communities, and social structures. They were not one unified country.</p>
      <p>After the Seven Years' War ended in 1763, the British government faced significant debts and tried to raise revenue from its colonies. Measures such as the Stamp Act of 1765 increased tensions. Many colonists argued that Parliament should not tax them without their consent or representation. Others remained loyal to the British Crown, and many people did not support a break with Britain.</p>
      <p>Protests, boycotts, political arguments, and confrontations followed. In 1774, representatives from twelve colonies met at the First Continental Congress. Fighting began at Lexington and Concord in April 1775. The conflict had started, but independence had not yet been formally declared.</p>
<p>One earlier confrontation became a powerful symbol of colonial resistance. On December 16, 1773, protesters in Boston boarded ships and dumped East India Company tea into the harbor. The protest became known as the Boston Tea Party. It was one episode in a much wider dispute over taxation, political authority, and the relationship between the colonies and Britain.</p>
