    <section class="lesson-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">04 · IDEALS AND REALITY</p>
      <h2>Who was included in the promise of freedom?</h2>
      <p>The Declaration's language about equality and rights became central to American political culture. Yet in 1776, slavery remained legal in every colony that became a state, women did not have equal political rights, and Native nations faced pressure, conflict, dispossession, and the loss of land as the new republic expanded.</p>
      <p>People challenged these contradictions in different ways. Enslaved and free Black people petitioned, resisted, escaped, and served in the war. Women organized boycotts, managed businesses and farms, wrote political arguments, and contributed to the revolutionary effort, while remaining excluded from formal political power. Native nations made their own diplomatic and military decisions in a conflict that threatened their homelands.</p>
      <p>Understanding the founding era means studying both the ideals people proclaimed and the unequal realities people lived. The history of the United States includes generations of debate and struggle over who could enjoy the rights described in its founding documents.</p>
      <p class="ru">Язык равенства и прав стал важной частью американской политической культуры. Но в 1776 году рабство сохранялось во всех колониях, ставших штатами; женщины не обладали равными политическими правами; коренные народы сталкивались с давлением, войнами и потерей земель. История основания США — это одновременно история провозглашённых идеалов и борьба за то, чтобы эти идеалы распространялись на большее число людей.</p>
    </section>

