      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">07 · A SHORT TIMELINE</p>
      <h2>From protest to a constitutional republic</h2>
      <div class="timeline" aria-label="Timeline of the founding of the United States">
        <article><span>1763</span><div><h3>Seven Years' War ends</h3><p>Britain faces war debts and new tensions with its colonies.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1765</span><div><h3>The Stamp Act</h3><p>A new tax on printed materials intensifies colonial opposition to taxation without representation.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1773</span><div><h3>Boston Tea Party</h3><p>Protesters destroy tea in Boston Harbor, turning resistance into a dramatic public confrontation.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1774</span><div><h3>First Continental Congress</h3><p>Delegates from twelve colonies meet to coordinate their response to British policies.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1775</span><div><h3>Armed conflict begins</h3><p>Fighting breaks out at Lexington and Concord in April.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1776</span><div><h3>Declaration of Independence</h3><p>Congress adopts the Declaration on July 4, announcing the colonies' independence.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1783</span><div><h3>Treaty of Paris</h3><p>Britain formally recognizes American independence, bringing the Revolutionary War to an end.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1787</span><div><h3>The Constitution is drafted</h3><p>Delegates in Philadelphia agree on a new framework for the federal government.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1788</span><div><h3>The Constitution is ratified</h3><p>The required number of states approves the new Constitution.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1789</span><div><h3>New federal government begins</h3><p>The Constitution takes effect and the new government starts operating.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1791</span><div><h3>Bill of Rights</h3><p>The first ten amendments are ratified, protecting a range of individual liberties.</p></div></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-section language-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">AMERICAN ENGLISH · KEY VOCABULARY</p>
      <h2>Words to understand and use</h2>
      <div class="vocab-grid">
        <article><strong>colony</strong><span>колония</span><p>A territory controlled by another country.</p></article>
        <article><strong>to declare independence</strong><span>провозгласить независимость</span><p>To officially announce that a country is independent.</p></article>
        <article><strong>representation</strong><span>представительство</span><p>Having someone speak or act on your behalf in government.</p></article>
        <article><strong>to ratify</strong><span>ратифицировать</span><p>To formally approve an agreement or document.</p></article>
        <article><strong>to establish</strong><span>учредить, создать</span><p>To create something intended to last or operate.</p></article>
        <article><strong>individual rights</strong><span>права личности</span><p>Rights and freedoms that belong to each person.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="phrase"><strong>Natural expression:</strong> “have a say” — to have the opportunity to express an opinion or influence a decision.<br /><span>Пример: “People wanted to have a say in how they were governed.” — Люди хотели иметь возможность влиять на то, как ими управляют.</span></div>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-section practice-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING</p>
      <h2>Think, describe, and speak</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Why did tensions grow between Britain and the thirteen colonies?</li>
        <li>What did the Declaration of Independence announce — and what did it not create?</li>
        <li>Why did the new country need a Constitution?</li>
        <li>Why was the Bill of Rights added?</li>
        <li>Which groups did not enjoy equal rights in the founding era?</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="speaking-prompt">Speaking challenge: Explain the difference between declaring independence and establishing a government in four or five sentences. Try to use <em>independence</em>, <em>representation</em>, <em>ratify</em>, and <em>individual rights</em>.</p>
      <p class="ru">Задание: объясните в четырёх-пяти предложениях разницу между провозглашением независимости и созданием системы государственного управления. Постарайтесь использовать новые английские слова.</p>
    </section>

